$DIR/cache/cache.p:12:2: an element of a map[string]int is written here with no lock that guards it, while another task may use it
    an element of a map[string]int is written at cache.p:12 in (*Cache).Put by
    the task started by task work at main.p:13 with no lock held, and the task
    started by task work at main.p:14 writes it at cache.p:12 in (*Cache).Put.
    Two tasks may run these at the same time, one of them writing, which is a
    data race: the other may see a value half written, or lose an update.
    The compiler infers which lock guards shared data from the locks held where
    it is written: a lock that every write holds. Here no one lock is held at
    every write. `return c.m` at cache.p:19 copies the map out of `Cache.m`; its
    contents are now one datum for all map[string]int values, which no lock of
    the struct that held the map guards.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold one sync.Mutex at every access, writes and reads (a sync.RWMutex's
    RLock is enough for reads):
        mu.Lock()
        m[k] = v
        mu.Unlock()
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3502" for a longer explanation)
